WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.280
I honestly don’t know how it&nbsp;
works in other countries, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:09.600
here in Italy for each apartment block there’s&nbsp;
an administrator who organizes general meetings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:13.680
and manages all the paperwork. All&nbsp;
right, now, imagine a condominium.&nbsp;

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:19.760
A big condominium, with 1 hundred and ninty three&nbsp;
193 families that, every year, as in all condos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:25.600
gather in an assembly. They discuss and&nbsp;
approve budgets, vote for their chairmen, argue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:31.040
and complain to each other about their neighbors.
But this Assembly has some peculiarities: in fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:34.160
every decision, report, recommendation&nbsp;
approved here is in no way binding.&nbsp;

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:40.480
Even if you convince the entire building that your&nbsp;
neighbor, Mr. city of Pistoia, has taken too many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:46.400
liberties in the use of common spaces, nothing&nbsp;
can stop him from carrying on as he pleases.&nbsp;

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:50.880
Only community decisions regarding&nbsp;
formal and bureaucratic matters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:56.400
expenses and budgets have any validity.
After all, that's what counts: money is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:01.120
no joke, and you know how it goes:&nbsp;
no one rejoices at the idea of being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:07.680
administrator and president of an apartment block.
Add another element: in addition to this Assembly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:13.840
there is another, more restricted and binding&nbsp;
one: a Council, whose authority, with all due&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:19.600
respect to Mr. Pistoia, is not in question.
There are ten families established by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:25.280
Assembly, which alternate for the next two years.&nbsp;
But to these another five families are added,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:30.320
and these always have their place in the&nbsp;
Council. They will say that they built the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:35.920
building and therefore...well they own it.
These are very special guys and, in fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:40.960
these 5 do not even trust each other: they&nbsp;
have studied a mechanism according to which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:46.640
if even one of them is against a resolution,&nbsp;
the resolution itself can be blocked.&nbsp;

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:52.880
This means that if one of them, let's say Mr.&nbsp;
Vladimir, decides to park his new flaming car in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:58.640
front of the garage of another of his colleague,&nbsp;
Mr. Volodymyr, this Council cannot do anything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:03.520
because any decision would require the&nbsp;
favorable vote of Mr. Vladimir himself.&nbsp;

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:11.040
Now, replace "condominium" with UN, family units&nbsp;
with "states", Mr. Pistoia with any forgotten&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:16.080
country [add Stan Countries] and awesome car with&nbsp;
Russian tank and we are already well on our way.&nbsp;

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:20.560
As for Mr. Vladimir and Mr. Volodymyr,&nbsp;
there is no need to change anything.&nbsp;

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:25.280
We need it to get straight to the question&nbsp;
of the day: what's wrong with the UN? On the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:29.680
one hand we have become accustomed to&nbsp;
the idea that the decisions that count&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:37.200
are always taken by the usual 5, the so-called&nbsp;
permanent members of the UN: USA, Russia, France,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:42.000
UK and the People's Republic of China.
This is the exclusive group, the private&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:46.720
party that can always get away with it.
There are proposals for a Security Council&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:52.000
more representative of the world population,&nbsp;
or suggestions to extend the permanent seat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:57.680
to Brazil, India and Germany, or even to&nbsp;
turn the French seat into a larger EU seat.&nbsp;

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:02.160
But for the moment we have to make do with&nbsp;
the compromise of the non-permanent members,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:06.560
provided that your country is lucky enough to&nbsp;
end up there at the right time. This does not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:12.320
undermine the fact that there is a considerable&nbsp;
gap between the competences of the Assembly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:17.680
and those of the Council, the only body that&nbsp;
can deliberate sanctions, embargoes and even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:23.200
military actions against possible aggressors.
It is true, however, that according to Article&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:27.920
27 of the UN Charter, two different&nbsp;
majorities are established each time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:32.000
depending on the two types of issues to&nbsp;
be submitted to the vote of the council.&nbsp;

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:37.920
There are procedural matters, i.e. the formal&nbsp;
ones that establish the course of the session,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:44.080
which require the favorable vote of at least&nbsp;
9 members, both permanent and non-permanent.&nbsp;

00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:49.760
And then there are the decisions "on any other&nbsp;
matter" which always require a majority of nine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:56.400
votes, including those of the 5 permanent members.
In a certain respect, article 27 recognizes the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.400 --> 00:04:02.720
superiority of some states over all the others.
And here we already have a first jarring note:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:08.560
it is up to the Council alone, or rather to the&nbsp;
5 permanents, to establish what is a threat to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:14.000
peace, an aggression or an act of conquest.
In some cases there is not much to discuss,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:19.600
such as the invasion of Kuwait in 1991 by&nbsp;
Saddam's Iraq: on that occasion resolutions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:25.200
were issued calling for the withdrawal of&nbsp;
Iraqi troops, imposing sanctions and finally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:31.120
with resolution 678, authorizing the&nbsp;
use of force to impose Iraqi withdrawal.&nbsp;

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:36.560
But it doesn't always work smoothly, see what&nbsp;
happened in Bosnia and Somalia. In a sense,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:43.280
rather than banning wars, the Security Council is&nbsp;
the only body that can recognize a state's right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:48.560
to wage wars. Things would become much more&nbsp;
complicated if the bad guy was one of the 5&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:52.880
permanent members, and here we have the&nbsp;
second and main problem: the so-called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:58.320
vetoes. Actually the power of veto is not&nbsp;
explicitly mentioned in the UN Charter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:04.080
and it is not a veto in a strict sense. According&nbsp;
to the already mentioned article 27, for all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:10.080
the issues that are not procedural, therefore&nbsp;
issues related to peace, conflict resolution,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:15.600
sanctions and even the entry of countries in&nbsp;
the UN, the nine minimum votes in favor must&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:21.760
include the approval of all 5 permanent members,&nbsp;
and therefore the contrary vote of one of them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.760 --> 00:05:28.880
implicitly becomes a kind of veto. In reality,&nbsp;
the negative opinion of one of the 5 cannot block&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:33.840
the deliberation from the beginning, as a pure&nbsp;
veto right would allow, but it can only make it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:39.360
void during the voting phase. Similarly, it must&nbsp;
be said, even the non-permanent members enjoy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:46.960
in theory, a similar power, the so-called&nbsp;
block veto. Even if all 5 vote in favor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.960 --> 00:05:53.360
there would still be 4 votes missing to reach&nbsp;
a majority of 9. If the non-permanent members&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.360 --> 00:06:01.120
would unite to vote against, well the party’s&nbsp;
compromised. For convenience, as everyone does,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.120 --> 00:06:06.880
I will call this prerogative by the term veto,&nbsp;
even though it would be a misnomer for some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.880 --> 00:06:11.920
lawyers. We would then have the paradox of an&nbsp;
aggressor country sitting comfortably on its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.920 --> 00:06:18.240
seat in the Council that finds itself having to&nbsp;
vote on the resolution condemning its own actions:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:24.080
In your opinion, will this nation block itself&nbsp;
with its veto, or will it play dumb and forget&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:31.040
to vote against it? The answer is pretty clear.&nbsp;
Yet, such rebel nation must still participate in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.040 --> 00:06:37.040
the discussion. At least to get the scolding from&nbsp;
the other four. This game of "veto not veto" has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.040 --> 00:06:43.680
already been cleared and abused in the 70s by&nbsp;
the UN members, so nothing shocking. It was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:49.040
Soviet Union that got into it right away.&nbsp;
However, Stalin, in his "infinite" wisdom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.040 --> 00:06:54.240
decided to boycott the sessions of the Council&nbsp;
when its members continued to recognize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.240 --> 00:07:02.160
Taiwan rather than Mao's China. In the meantime,&nbsp;
in 1950, North Korea had invaded South Korea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:06.800
and the Security Council, in a hurry and taking&nbsp;
advantage of the Soviet absence, approved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.800 --> 00:07:12.000
resolutions on which the Soviet Union would&nbsp;
certainly have vetoed. From that moment on, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:17.920
Soviet Union did not skip any sessions, and put&nbsp;
its veto on everything possible. But don't worry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.920 --> 00:07:23.040
the other 4 didn’t stand by and watch. United&nbsp;
Kingdom and France used the right of veto for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:28.880
first time in 1956, during the Suez crisis that&nbsp;
saw them involved, together with Israel, in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.880 --> 00:07:35.600
military occupation of the canal against Egypt.&nbsp;
Then in the 70's it was the turn of the United&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.600 --> 00:07:41.600
States, which exercised its right of veto mainly&nbsp;
in defense of Israel from the risk of sanctions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:48.880
The USSR loved the use of the veto so much that&nbsp;
Ambassador Andrei Gromyko earned the nickname of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.880 --> 00:07:58.080
Mr. Nyet (Mr. No), while Vyacheslav Molotov that&nbsp;
of Mr. Veto. We did the math: the veto right has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.080 --> 00:08:04.640
been exercised two hundred and sixty three times,&nbsp;
sometimes by several members together, for 213&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.640 --> 00:08:13.280
rejected resolutions. Russia/Urss for 120 times,&nbsp;
then United States at 82, United Kingdom 29,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.280 --> 00:08:19.600
France 16, and China 16. The veto right has&nbsp;
often been used as a kind of protection for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.600 --> 00:08:24.480
one's own interests, something that has little&nbsp;
to do with achieving peace and harmony. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.480 --> 00:08:28.880
here we can open the cauldron of contradictions.&nbsp;
The UN is supposed to be an equal organization,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.880 --> 00:08:35.600
yet 5 countries can block everything. And okay,&nbsp;
those 5 are the winners of WWII, but can France&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.600 --> 00:08:41.595
and UK still be considered great powers as in&nbsp;
the Eighteenth century? It was Roosevelt at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.595 --> 00:08:47.680
time who named 4 policemen to ensure peace in the&nbsp;
world; the three war winners plus the Republic of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.680 --> 00:08:53.440
China, despite the fact that the latter was in&nbsp;
a political crisis, according to a concept that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.440 --> 00:08:59.200
was actually very close to the classic divisions&nbsp;
by "spheres of influence". And let's take into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:05.200
account that dozens and dozens of vetoes have been&nbsp;
used not to prevent international crises, but to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.200 --> 00:09:10.880
hinder the appointment of Secretaries General,&nbsp;
or worse, to prevent the entry of some states&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.880 --> 00:09:16.320
into the UN. Like children in primary school. The&nbsp;
People's Republic of China replaced the Taiwanese&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.320 --> 00:09:22.960
in 1971 and immediately resorted to vetoes&nbsp;
to prevent the entry of Bangladesh and other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.960 --> 00:09:30.160
countries that were still recognizing Taiwan. We&nbsp;
Italians managed to enter the United Nations only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.160 --> 00:09:36.960
in 1955, due to vetoes placed by the Soviet Union.&nbsp;
This management system thus seems to grant the 5&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.960 --> 00:09:43.120
powerful permanent members that no decision they&nbsp;
do not like will ever be effective. The veto power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.120 --> 00:09:49.280
somehow implies that nothing can be done without&nbsp;
the total willingness of the big 5. Basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.280 --> 00:09:55.920
The world cannot be at peace if they 5 are not&nbsp;
at peace. The underlying assumption of the veto&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.920 --> 00:10:02.480
mechanism thus seems to be that world order is not&nbsp;
possible if the big 5 do not agree all together.&nbsp;

00:10:02.480 --> 00:10:08.080
As if to say: if sanctions on Lukashenko may&nbsp;
upset one of us (who knows who -- PUTIN), with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.080 --> 00:10:13.040
the risk of creating tensions between us,&nbsp;
then better not to sanction him at all.&nbsp;

00:10:13.040 --> 00:10:18.960
Rather than a real blockade, some see the veto&nbsp;
as a sort of prevention, a collective mechanism:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.960 --> 00:10:23.760
it is a way to ensure that a conflict does&nbsp;
not break out among the 5 permanent members,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.760 --> 00:10:27.840
which apparently, would be the most&nbsp;
important thing for our global stability.&nbsp;

00:10:27.840 --> 00:10:32.880
There are also those who argue that the absence&nbsp;
of the veto would create more instability,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.880 --> 00:10:38.720
and so it would be right to keep such veto. Vetoes&nbsp;
proved their validity only in cases that were of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.720 --> 00:10:44.080
narrow interest to the Council itself and&nbsp;
most of the times vetoes weren’t used to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.080 --> 00:10:50.080
meet humanitarian demands or to put an end to&nbsp;
international conflicts. And so Russia vetoed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.080 --> 00:10:55.680
the resolution to open an investigation into&nbsp;
the Malaysian Airlines accident in Donbass,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.680 --> 00:11:03.200
as well as the condemnation for the annexation of&nbsp;
Crimea. Russia also vetoed, together with China,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.200 --> 00:11:09.600
the resolutions against the Assad regime in&nbsp;
Syria in 2011 and 2012. Just as on the other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.600 --> 00:11:15.280
side of the table, the U.S. vetoed the recognition&nbsp;
of the genocide in Rwanda. In addition to this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.280 --> 00:11:21.200
many says that the continued US vetoes against&nbsp;
sanctions targeting Israel are contributing to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.200 --> 00:11:27.360
exacerbate relations with the Palestinians.&nbsp;
Mentioning Syria, aside from Russia and Iran,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.360 --> 00:11:32.560
the big allies who have supported its bombings&nbsp;
and attacks, the United States, Saudi Arabia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.560 --> 00:11:39.840
EAU and Turkey have also been part of the clashes.&nbsp;
And just as Russia has violated the UN Charte,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.840 --> 00:11:45.200
bombing the Aleppo region and hundreds of civilian&nbsp;
targets, as well as approving the use of chemical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.200 --> 00:11:50.800
weapons by the Assad regime, the United&nbsp;
States of Obama and Trump did the same thing:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.800 --> 00:11:56.000
By launching more than fifty Tomahawk cruise&nbsp;
missiles on Syria, the Americans bypassed the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.000 --> 00:12:01.280
constraints of the UN Charter that limit&nbsp;
the use of force in international affairs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.280 --> 00:12:06.720
And in the end, the UN Security Council still&nbsp;
could not agree on a joint statement on Syria.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.720 --> 00:12:12.640
Russia, has repeatedly blocked negotiations moved&nbsp;
by the other three Western nations. For their own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.640 --> 00:12:17.680
part, the United Kingdom, France and the United&nbsp;
States have done the same with Russian demands,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.680 --> 00:12:24.320
calling them excessive and unacceptable. And so&nbsp;
the endless game continues. So it’s pretty normal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.320 --> 00:12:30.320
to expected that a Council structured in this way&nbsp;
is going to be unable to take action in conflicts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.320 --> 00:12:36.080
of global dimensions, right because such conflicts&nbsp;
may involve a permanent member. For days the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.080 --> 00:12:40.960
UN members have been discussing a possible&nbsp;
suspension or expulsion of Russia from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.960 --> 00:12:46.400
UN and the Security Council, in order to bypass&nbsp;
its veto in the resolutions concerning Ukraine.&nbsp;

00:12:46.400 --> 00:12:49.200
Apart from the fact that the&nbsp;
possible veto of China would still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.200 --> 00:12:54.880
remain, is it really possible to exclude Russia?
A first step has already been taken in this sense,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.880 --> 00:13:00.240
with the suspension of Moscow from the UN&nbsp;
Human Rights Council at least until 2023,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.240 --> 00:13:03.840
an action made possible by the&nbsp;
decision of the General Assembly alone.&nbsp;

00:13:03.840 --> 00:13:08.480
Only Libya suffered a similar fate&nbsp;
in 2011, but here with Russia we are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.480 --> 00:13:12.720
dealing with a member of the Council.
Beyond its symbolic significance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.720 --> 00:13:18.160
this measure denies Russia the possibility&nbsp;
to propose resolutions and amendments in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.160 --> 00:13:23.600
situations in which it is not directly involved.
Suspending Russia from the UN and therefore&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.600 --> 00:13:28.080
from the Security Council, however, is more&nbsp;
complicated and according to many analists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.080 --> 00:13:33.840
actually impossible. According to article 5,&nbsp;
the Assembly could decide, with two thirds of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:33.840 --> 00:13:39.200
votes in favor, the suspension of a UN country,&nbsp;
but only towards those countries towards which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.200 --> 00:13:45.840
the Security Council has initiated proceedings.
In other words, it is necessary to ask Russia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.840 --> 00:13:52.240
first if it is okay to be suspended. The very&nbsp;
borderline hypothesis of denying the Russian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:52.240 --> 00:13:56.560
delegation the credentials to represent&nbsp;
its country, from documentation to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.560 --> 00:14:01.040
recognition of its delegates, was then discussed.
This is a procedure that is normally implemented&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:01.040 --> 00:14:06.560
for those countries where a coup d'état or civil&nbsp;
war undermines the legitimacy of the government&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.560 --> 00:14:11.760
and consequently of the UN delegation. This&nbsp;
happened recently in the cases of Afghanistan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.760 --> 00:14:17.680
Myanmar and Cambodia in 1997. however&nbsp;
Russia does not have a similar situation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.680 --> 00:14:23.600
but it is clear that the intervention in Ukraine,&nbsp;
according to the UN chart (which by the way no one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.600 --> 00:14:29.520
seems to respect anymore), is a clear violation&nbsp;
of international law. But according to some,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.520 --> 00:14:34.880
it would be possible to exclude Russia even though&nbsp;
it does not have a coup d'état or a civil war:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.880 --> 00:14:41.600
the same thing had already been done with regard&nbsp;
to South Africa, in 1974. At first, the Security&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:41.600 --> 00:14:47.120
Council rejected a resolution to suspend the&nbsp;
African country, requested as a measure in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:47.120 --> 00:14:49.600
response to the apartheid regime. Vetoed by the&nbsp;
United States, France and the United Kingdom, in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.600 --> 00:14:58.160
case you were wondering. Nevertheless, the General&nbsp;
Assembly symbolically refused to approve the South&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:58.160 --> 00:15:03.440
African credentials, to the point of deciding to&nbsp;
no longer admit the south african delegation to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:03.440 --> 00:15:09.600
the Assembly, at least until 1994 with the end of&nbsp;
apartheid. And here we should stress the sentence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.600 --> 00:15:15.200
"dont' try this at home". Maybe the safest way&nbsp;
to solve the UN dilemma is for the suspension&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:15.200 --> 00:15:20.080
to mature within the Council itself, which is&nbsp;
obviously impossible at the moment. Finally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:20.080 --> 00:15:25.120
the question we can't help but ask ourselves&nbsp;
is who knows what a Chavez would have done,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:25.120 --> 00:15:30.800
with his little guitar... Or Gaddafi with his&nbsp;
uninterrupted monologue of an hour and a half...&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:30.800 --> 00:15:35.520
perhaps to find a way out of this quagmire&nbsp;
it takes just the right amount of madness.

